{"step_index":920,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T20:54:58Z","input_tokens":2536,"cache_read_tokens":349075,"output_tokens":793,"thinking":"I'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\nThe JavaScript code within the HTML files is easily readable, revealing fetching and decoding of remote URLs. The structure suggests a focus on obfuscation. The user's query about encryption reinforces the need to harden the JavaScript code.\n\n\nInvestigating the current structure of `index.html` and `teacher.html` is crucial after modification. Researching effective JavaScript protection methods for browser HTML is underway, with a focus on evaluating each layer's resilience against AI analysis. Comparing architectural layering versus JS obfuscation, encryption, and runtime evaluation is necessary to provide clear options.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"javascript-obfuscator control flow flattening rc4 string encryption ai analysis resistance"}},{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"self decrypting html javascript eval cipher payload offline browser file protocol"}}]}
{"step_index":921,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T20:55:07Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T04:55:07+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T04:55:13+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T04:55:07+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T04:55:13+08:00\nThe search for \"javascript-obfuscator control flow flattening rc4 string encryption ai analysis resistance\" returned the following summary:\nThe combination of **Control Flow Flattening**, **RC4 String Encryption**, and **AI Analysis Resistance** represents a modern, high-security hardening standard for JavaScript applications (commonly implemented via enterprise pipelines or tools like `javascript-obfuscator`[1] and custom VM wrappers[2]). \n\nThis architecture makes static analysis by humans and automated AI agents exceptionally difficult[3]. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Core Mechanics: How the Protection Layers Work\n\n#### A. String Encryption (RC4 / Base64 Array Rotation)\n* **The Mechanism:** Instead of leaving literal strings (like API endpoints, error messages, or secret keys) exposed in plaintext, the obfuscator extracts them into a centralized \"string array\"[1]. \n* **The Layering:** It encodes them using algorithms like Base64 or **RC4 symmetric encryption**, often coupled with an index-shuffling or runtime rotation routine[4]. \n* **The Runtime Effect:** A self-invoking function (IIFE) initializes the array, uses a dynamic key (sometimes derived from the environment or code structure) to decrypt strings on-the-fly, and maps them back via proxy lookups. Static scanners see only gibberish byte arrays.\n\n#### B. Control Flow Flattening (CFF)\n* **The Mechanism:** CFF destroys the natural hierarchical flow of code (if/else statements, loops, sequential blocks)[5]. It chops a function up into isolated \"basic blocks\" and places them inside an infinite `while` loop governed by a massive `switch` statement[6][5].\n* **The Dispatcher:** A state variable tracks which block should execute next. At the end of each block, the state variable is mathematically updated (often obfuscated with arithmetic operations) so the `switch` dispatcher routes execution to the next logical block[7].\n* **The Structural Impact:** The Control Flow Graph (CFG) changes from a readable tree structure into a dense, flat star-like web where every block points back to the central dispatcher, blinding traditional de-obfuscators[5].\n\n#### C. AI Analysis Resistance (Thwarting LLMs)\nModern reverse engineering often relies on Large Language Models (LLMs) to de-obfuscate code snippets. Protections defeat AI analysis through several vectors:\n* **Context Window Exhaustion:** Control flow flattening exponentially increases token size and code verbosity, forcing complex scripts past an LLM's effective context or reasoning limits.\n* **Opaque Predicates & Dead Code Injection:** The inclusion of fake code branches and mathematically convoluted invariants tricks AI into chasing \"hallucinated\" execution paths.\n* **Symbol and Logic Mismatches:** Because variable names are mangled into hex strings (`_0x3bfc5c`) and business logic is fragmented across a state machine, semantic clues required for an AI to infer *intent* are systematically stripped[1][6].\n\n---\n\n### 2. De-obfuscation & Analysis Challenges\n\nWhen an analyst (or an AI agent) encounters this triad, they face specific barriers:\n\n1. **Static Analysis Failure:** Looking at the source file yields no plaintext strings (thanks to RC4) and no linear function logic (thanks to CFF). An LLM cannot simply \"read\" the code to understand what it does because the execution order is abstracted into numerical state transitions.\n2. **Dynamic Instrumentation Traps:** To see the RC4 strings or bypass the CFF state machine, analysts often resort to runtime debugging (e.g., Puppeteer, CDP, or browser DevTools)[3]. However, production-grade obfuscation packages frequently layer **Anti-Debugging**, **Anti-Tampering**, and **VM Self-Defending** scripts that detect breakpoints, inspect call stacks, or corrupt execution if inspection is detected[3].\n\n---\n\n### 3. De-obfuscation Strategies (The Analyst's Playbook)\n\nOvercoming this level of protection requires a systematic, multi-step de-obfuscation pipeline:\n\n* **AST-Based De-flattening:** Tools built on Abstract Syntax Trees (using libraries like Babel or Esprima/Estraverse) are written to symbolically evaluate the state variables. If the state transitions can be statically resolved, the `switch-case` blocks can be re-sequenced back into linear, readable JavaScript.\n* **Emulation & Symbolic Execution:** Instead of full static analysis, running the initialization and string-decryption routines in a safe, sandboxed Node.js environment allows an analyst to intercept and dump the decrypted string array once the RC4 routine finishes executing.\n* **Taint Analysis & Hooking:** By proxying native JavaScript objects or hooking key runtime functions, analysts log inputs and outputs of the string lookup functions, bypassing the need to mathematically reverse the RC4 cipher.\n\nSources:\n[1] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFaZiwHA5kU-61M6rp08jHW6DvL3095yuLE2f0n6u7sWe2clUgwPdMJ5D-wUTslf4_dh53lTFx2vVr7GqdHCli6nuisp-Vjb8H_MjZG28FUEZp_J2xAPrnNdqwthcf_C84vd8s1ygkb9DiWZJMTamRGg5mWNUQ5WGzuIsqOFLI3BjwTmKtMpuKCQeY=)\n[2] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEP6VRkhsIr9KYUGPtu7brZCaw_fte0AwnsDR20vVffFtBWndFqsD2XsiZwY6IobilRNfuwlS1wKboPHDmNti2SGIggG7XZxGgaqloH9x_aaCEiHa7-QSgUMYxytXPNf230L0COOAjHJvJZNWF1Hwtx6IRGww==)\n[3] [obfuscator.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFxc5AxcjTIkwFkbNYxCTWG00-W7KWz2AGpHau1_R9iC63-15rxmdbkuY4nbIpTI-JRddvh1o2LFP0PYW5Lb9YcMpFmS8KmmNa3LbS0)\n[4] [medium.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHc4KO8ZejWdKA2E-IQ7u1ZfNTk7kDaPKoVt7LPok5KzmIbaxRdwhcWskGk9mc87X9hRwnVrWRbmZz8_PY0WP-zfH1Y8Hv164uRoDCq_-xnroaIX_wHo3N6n9t4TZAy1fMuLW-_XCHVMhFWKVwjQtXxxr_bZrdjTi2AhfncabyfurFzcGVZVN2HVAMmiRO0FloUiacYF8cFzX5nzoB4)\n[5] [jscrambler.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEMI01qn9Zt7NMbOVp0pDDnD_IkNwpzfELXjiG73c3LVkfm2zRFkpzpWf_HFiRFB-hw0-6iMC0wvnxAPf1km4qs5R2zwqdduoNUADAkzlq1xk77gtDJobzB3GIzXigRNZfbs8wpot1b-sVEj1d8POjYANbEzsBYchb8)\n[6] [obfuscator.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHIkCPXSM9CpPPFa5TTuef5uU2jWW-sIhrSQFJ-xEbLCgjP9QNY-1DBPZMPdfsf5qnclQk2kQWNYLq8Yo2-pwzaaXRbnFnFxf1VGb1pOUG6PUDPs3KmDIaN)\n[7] [stackexchange.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGp8IkhZ7ZUVNtzMoa4zCdUc6ikbXcBRTtCOlHb9ZlOCffrNezlXj9wlVYRvTsFVpQn08FptildN2lk38YYOEqTZwQB_gD7Ep0d7spz_UB15-m1h5ecpeppzNJk23c2vTQMAvqrJD2CEECE4ka3wWf96UupiV5P06M0i71O-boJnvpdDm4QMwC_HIE5-NgyxIvcErsmhVAccelkV6dF-RFyaxGPnil7trc5)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
{"step_index":922,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T20:55:07Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T04:55:07+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T04:55:19+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T04:55:07+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T04:55:19+08:00\nThe search for \"self decrypting html javascript eval cipher payload offline browser file protocol\" returned the following summary:\nA **self-decrypting HTML payload** running via the local `file://` protocol relies entirely on client-side execution. Because it operates completely offline without a backend server, it packages an encrypted data payload alongside a plain JavaScript bootstrap routine[1][2]. When loaded into a browser, the bootstrap script decrypts the payload in memory and injects or executes it[3].\n\n---\n\n### Core Mechanics\n\n1. **The Wrapper (HTML + JS):** A clean structural wrapper containing minimal bootstrap JavaScript[3].\n2. **The Ciphertext:** The core payload (HTML, JavaScript, or application code) encoded and encrypted using a symmetric cipher (e.g., AES-GCM) stored directly inside a `<script>` or `<template>` tag[2].\n3. **The Key / Password:** Can be hardcoded into the bootstrap script (for obfuscation/local-only defense) or prompted from the user via a password box at runtime[2].\n4. **The Execution (`eval` / DOM Injection):** Once decrypted in memory, the payload is dynamically written to the DOM or executed via `eval()` / `Function()` constructor execution blocks[3].\n\n---\n\n### Conceptual Code Architecture\n\nBelow is a proof-of-concept demonstrating how a standalone HTML file can use the modern browser **Web Crypto API** (`crypto.subtle`) and AES-GCM to self-decrypt and execute an encrypted payload offline, triggered locally via the `file://` protocol.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <title>Self-Decrypting Offline Container</title>\n    <style>\n        body { font-family: sans-serif; background: #121212; color: #e0e0e0; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; height: 100vh; margin: 0; }\n        #loader { text-align: center; }\n        input, button { padding: 10px; margin-top: 10px; background: #222; border: 1px solid #444; color: #fff; border-radius: 4px; }\n        button { cursor: pointer; background: #333; }\n        button:hover { background: #444; }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n    <div id=\"loader\">\n        <h3>Protected Content</h3>\n        <p>Enter decryption passphrase:</p>\n        <input type=\"password\" id=\"password\" placeholder=\"Passphrase\" />\n        <br>\n        <button onclick=\"decryptPayload()\">Decrypt & Execute</button>\n        <p id=\"error\" style=\"color: #ff6b6b;\"></p>\n    </div>\n\n    <div id=\"content\" style=\"display:none;\"></div>\n\n    <script>\n        // --- CONFIGURATION & EMBEDDED CIPHERTEXT ---\n        // In a real build pipeline, these variables are populated automatically.\n        // Below are placeholders representing base64-encoded cryptographic parameters.\n        \n        const saltB64 = \"MOCK_BASE64_SALT_DATA\";\n        const ivB64 = \"MOCK_BASE64_IV_DATA\";\n        const ciphertextB64 = \"MOCK_BASE64_CIPHERTEXT_DATA\";\n\n        // Helper: Convert Base64 to Uint8Array\n        function base64ToArrayBuffer(base64) {\n            const binary_string = window.atob(base64);\n            const len = binary_string.length;\n            const bytes = new Uint8Array(len);\n            for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n                bytes[i] = binary_string.charCodeAt(i);\n            }\n            return bytes.buffer;\n        }\n\n        async function deriveKey(password, salt) {\n            const enc = new TextEncoder();\n            const keyMaterial = await window.crypto.subtle.importKey(\n                \"raw\",\n                enc.encode(password),\n                { name: \"PBKDF2\" },\n                false,\n                [\"deriveKey\"]\n            );\n            return window.crypto.subtle.deriveKey(\n                {\n                    name: \"PBKDF2\",\n                    salt: salt,\n                    iterations: 100000,\n                    hash: \"SHA-256\"\n                },\n                keyMaterial,\n                { name: \"AES-GCM\", length: 256 },\n                false,\n                [\"decrypt\"]\n            );\n        }\n\n        async function decryptPayload() {\n            const password = document.getElementById('password').value;\n            const errorElement = document.getElementById('error');\n            errorElement.textContent = \"\";\n\n            try {\n                const salt = base64ToArrayBuffer(saltB64);\n                const iv = base64ToArrayBuffer(ivB64);\n                const data = base64ToArrayBuffer(ciphertextB64);\n\n                const key = await deriveKey(password, salt);\n\n                // Decrypt using Web Crypto API\n                const decryptedArrayBuffer = await window.crypto.subtle.decrypt(\n                    { name: \"AES-GCM\", iv: iv },\n                    key,\n                    data\n                );\n\n                const decoder = new TextDecoder();\n                const decryptedCode = decoder.decode(decryptedArrayBuffer);\n\n                // Clean up UI wrapper\n                document.getElementById('loader').style.display = 'none';\n                document.getElementById('content').style.display = 'block';\n\n                // --- EXECUTION PHASE ---\n                // Option A: If the payload is dynamic HTML/DOM structures\n                // document.getElementById('content').innerHTML = decryptedCode;\n\n                // Option B: If the payload demands code evaluation / script execution\n                // Using an explicit function wrapper or direct eval safely bound inside scope:\n                const runScript = new Function(decryptedCode);\n                runScript();\n\n            } catch (err) {\n                console.error(err);\n                errorElement.textContent = \"Decryption failed. Incorrect password or corrupted payload.\";\n            }\n        }\n    </script>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Important Limitations Under the `file://` Protocol\n\nWhen designing self-decrypting packages targeted at offline execution via the local file system (`file://`), browsers apply strict security boundaries:\n\n1. **CORS Restrictions on Local Imports:** \n   You cannot use external `fetch()` calls, external scripts (`<script src=\"...\">`), or local module imports (`import ... from ...`) relative to the file path in most modern browsers. **The file must remain entirely monolithic (self-contained)**, embedding all code, styles, and data inline.\n2. **Web Crypto API Requirements:** \n   The `window.crypto.subtle` library functions natively inside offline contexts (`file://`) across most modern browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari), meaning robust modern cryptography (AES-GCM, PBKDF2) is fully operational without requiring an HTTP/HTTPS web server context.\n3. **Content Security Policy (CSP) & `eval()`:** \n   If local HTML files are forced to parse raw strings via `eval()` or dynamic `Function` instantiations after runtime decryption, strict local browser configurations or enterprise policies might occasionally flag or block execution if a restrictive meta-CSP is applied. However, native single-file dynamic DOM writing (`element.innerHTML` or creating script elements dynamically via `document.createElement('script')`) usually bypasses static heuristic blocks because the malicious or sensitive source code is not exposed natively in the initial raw text layout of the document.\n\nSources:\n[1] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH50NnA5jetEO2o7b8JYV9cTYnIT0JNPQgWwLbrwc6Hv3BN5Dk0rZus8apn52_N-HqGZNTaPGaFlyaxSBVyLWnNSKVawtlVYF9wgDpXzQ7uoXToU-nXvO3NmAkj4zvBViAgVdM5oi8Hy3bXPqo=)\n[2] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG1WlwkIzmNg_1A686KtvA3jIAOjn67tHI08whonIBCVXPyC33lj0W0jIFVRQjpNjhavHNBX7XLUXKrG4daEUOVLPuUMULwSyUsfhLFxzzm7vuol77bDWWERQg1TyrTlQ==)\n[3] [html-code-generator.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHoWiwuhzxNCi-FzYJ1pCIUFdZPow1bJrcrUR3jyVOsNyv6m2qhoYeXQtZhmkhulK9iTlQpzXDsVjAW27H6S6ydgCeCVNZozalCBPnlAwHGgJy-b7aUJcKx7IumtJ34NpEO6su30mn3xfC-8epu-64eIBOfIw==)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
