he user's requirement for a visibly encrypted local database.\n\n\nThe primary concern is ensuring the compatibility of the `injectVault()` function in `teacher.html` with the proposed encryption method in `index.html`. Specifically, encrypting the entirety of `<script>` in `index.html` could prevent the injection of the encrypted vault token by `injectVault()` if the intended insertion point is swallowed by the encryption. It's critical to determine if the `LOCAL_ENCRYPTED_VAULT` placeholder can be maintained and updated correctly after script encryption.\n\n\nThe optimal approach involves embedding the encrypted data within a dedicated `<script>` tag in the HTML, avoiding fragile regex-based insertion. This dedicated tag, such as `<script id=\"local-db-vault\" type=\"application/octet-stream\"></script>`, will contain the encrypted payload. The encrypted runtime script will then retrieve the ciphertext from the dedicated script tag's content. This strategy simplifies injection, making the approach extremely robust and also provides a clear visual indicator of the encrypted local database.\n\n\nThe encryption strategy significantly hinders source code analysis, effectively thwarting casual code review and automated AI analysis by obfuscating core logic through layered techniques. However, it's crucial to acknowledge the limitations of client-side encryption; the browser's network activity remains visible and dynamic script sandboxing could potentially reveal request details.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"Get-ChildItem -Path . -Force | Select-Object Name, Length","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Check workspace root files"}}]}
